  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 2,
    "lesson_title": "Unit Step as an On-Off Switch",
    "lesson_core_move": "Interpret $x(t)$$u(t)$ as using the unit step as an on-off switch that suppresses a continuous-time signal before $t = 0 and passes it afterward$, supporting construction of started, delayed, and pulsed signals with unit steps.",
    "section_count": 4,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Unit Step as an On-Off Switch",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Unit Step as an On-Off Switch",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The object to recognize is the continuous-time unit step $u(t)$. In this topic, treat it as a switch: for $t<0$, it turns off; for $t\\ge 0$, it turns on. Therefore a product $y(t)=x(t)u(t)$ is a regionwise product that depends on the sign of $t$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=x(t)u(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the base case $x(t)=1$ so the switch action is transparent: then $y(t)=u(t)$. For any $x(t)$, apply the same rule and write the decomposition explicitly as a switched expression.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=\\begin{cases}0,\\; t<0\\\\x(t),\\; t\\ge 0\\end{cases}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The meaning is direct: $u(t)$ suppresses the signal before the switch time, so $y(t)=0$ for $t<0$, and it lets the original form pass unchanged afterward, so $y(t)=x(t)$ for $t\\ge 0$. This same template will be reused as the core move whenever step multiplication defines a region of activation.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Unit Step as an On-Off Switch",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Evaluating $x(t)$$u(t)$ Before $t = 0$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Evaluate $y(t)=x(t)u(t)$ for $y(t)=(2-t)u(t)$ at $t=-1$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Cue: this is an on-off switch evaluation in the pre-switch region. Use $y(t)=x(t)u(t)$ with $x(t)=2-t$ and evaluate both factors at $t=-1$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(-1)=(2-(-1))u(-1)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Because the switch is off in the region $t<0$, the value rule gives $u(-1)=0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "u(-1)=0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Substitute that factor into the product: $y(-1)=(2-(-1))\\cdot 0=0$. The result is $y(-1)=0$, which is exactly the explicit zero output in the blocked region.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Unit Step as an On-Off Switch",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Evaluating $x(t)$$u(t)$ After $t = 0$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Evaluate $y(t)=(3t+1)u(t)$ at $t=2$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Cue: this is an on-region evaluation where the gate is open. Identify the target expression as $y(t)=x(t)u(t)$ and substitute $t=2$ into both factors.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(2)=(3\\cdot2+1)u(2)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "From the switch rule, for $t>0$, $u(t)=1$, so here $u(2)=1$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "u(2)=1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now compute the switched value directly: $y(2)=(3\\cdot2+1)\\cdot 1=7$. This matches the active formula $x(2)=7$, so in the on region $y(t)=x(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Unit Step as an On-Off Switch",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Writing the On-Off Output of $x(t)$$u(t)$ in Piecewise Form",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Express $y(t)=(4t+1)u(t)$ as an explicit piecewise function of $t$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Cue: this is the same switch rule from earlier, now written for all times. Start from the generic product form $y(t)=x(t)u(t)$ with $x(t)=4t+1$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=x(t)u(t),\\quad x(t)=4t+1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Set the off branch using $t<0\\Rightarrow u(t)=0$, and the on branch using $t\\ge 0\\Rightarrow u(t)=1$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=\\begin{cases}0,\\; t<0 \\\\ 4t+1,\\; t\\ge 0\\end{cases}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This form is explicit for every time region: $y(t)$ is blocked to zero before the switch and equals $x(t)$ after and at the switch point, which is the core on-off behavior of $x(t)u(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
